\section{Error Analysis \emph{[Exploratory]}}
\label{sec:analysis}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_type_mechanism.pdf}
\caption{Type--mechanism association (percentage of violation traces per type assigned to each failure mechanism). Each type maps to a dominant mechanism ($\geq$3/5 model consistency), supporting the taxonomy's diagnostic value (NMI $= 0.23$, Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.45$, LLM-coded).}
\label{fig:type_mechanism}
\end{figure}

The following failure mode taxonomy emerges empirically from LLM-coded traces, distinct from the input ambiguity taxonomy (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}).

We analyze 614 violation traces across all five models, coded into five failure modes (Gwet's AC$_1$ $= 0.62$, $\kappa = 0.52$ \citep{gwet_2014,cohen_1960}); the coder prompt excludes ambiguity type labels, with type assigned post hoc. Using LLMs to code LLM failures risks circularity; however, type--mechanism mutual information ($V = 0.45$, bootstrap 95\% CI $[0.41, 0.50]$; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}) provides structural evidence against this.\footnote{$V$ is computed on a $6\times 5$ table vs.\ $6\times 2$ for severity; higher dimensionality makes $V = 0.45$ conservative.} Human mechanism coding yields $\kappa = 0.15$, rising to $0.34$--$0.52$ after collapsing the spurious-compliance/structural-misparse boundary (Appendix~\ref{app:human_validation}); we treat $V = 0.45$ as an upper bound. The following patterns should be interpreted as consistent LLM categorization tendencies rather than ground-truth assignments. Two of six mappings reflect partial definitional affinity (authorization scope$\to$boundary violation, incompleteness$\to$gap-filling); the remaining four (e.g., scopal$\to$structural misparse, 82.7\% consistency) are empirically derived.

\subsection{Type-Specific Failure Mechanisms}

The five-model data reveal three distinct failure patterns (as classified by LLM coders) that crosscut the specification/linguistic layer boundary.

\emph{Structural misparse}: the agent commits to an incorrect parse, paralleling garden-path processing \citep{frazier_rayner_1982,ferreira_good_enough_2002}. Dominates scopal (5/5), conditional precedence (4/5), and coreferential (3/5).

\emph{Information gap-filling}: the agent fills missing information with plausible but incorrect defaults. Dominates incompleteness (3/5) and lexical (3/5).

\emph{Boundary violation}: the agent exceeds permission boundaries when authorization scope is underspecified; 69.2\% involve unauthorized escalation with zero conservative refusals ($p < 10^{-4}$, binomial). Dominates authorization scope exclusively (5/5).

All dominant modes achieve $\geq$3/5 model consistency; scopal and authorization scope are unanimous (5/5).

\subsection{Disambiguation Neglect}
\label{sec:dn}

Agents rarely recognize ambiguity: overall awareness is 4.6\%, with GPT-4.1 and Claude at 10--11\% while Qwen3 and DeepSeek show zero ($p = 2.2 \times 10^{-9}$; Appendix~\ref{app:awareness_breakdown}). We term this \emph{disambiguation neglect}---extending ambiguity collapse \citep{liu_ambiguity_2023,hu_ambiguity_2025} from the representational level to the behavioral level: agents act on collapsed readings without recognizing ambiguity, producing irreversible safety violations rather than mere accuracy degradation. Systematic nocuity (\S\ref{sec:nocuity}) further extends to the population level. A prompt-based mitigation (Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}) confirms type-dependent effectiveness: authorization scope ($-$18.0\,pp), lexical ($-$8.9\,pp), but not incompleteness ($-$3.4\,pp, n.s.).

\subsection{Systematic Nocuity}
\label{sec:nocuity}

We adopt `nocuous' from \citet{berry_kamsties_2004}, but inverted: our agents exhibit \emph{convergent} rather than \emph{divergent} errors. Among ambiguous clauses, 41.3\% trigger majority-convergent violations ($\geq$3/5 models; 13.0\% unanimous)---we term this \emph{systematic nocuity}, invisible in agreement-based evaluation. These convergent violations exhibit a \emph{permissive attractor}: 77.0\% reflect permissive interpretation ($p \approx 0$; details in Appendix~\ref{app:attractor}).

\enlargethispage{26\baselineskip}

